Here’s a complete .gitignore template that works for Cursor (using .cursorignore syntax, which is identical to .gitignore) and also excludes Python virtual environments (venv, .env, etc.), along with common build artifacts.
You can use this for both .gitignore and .cursorignore.

.gitignore / .cursorignore Template
Gitignore# === Python Virtual Environments ===
venv/
.env/
env/
.venv/
ENV/
env.bak/
venv.bak/

# === Python Cache & Build Artifacts ===
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
*.pyd
*.pdb
*.py.class
*.egg
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
pip-wheel-metadata/
*.whl

# === IDE & Editor Files ===
.vscode/
.idea/
*.sublime-project
*.sublime-workspace

# === OS Generated Files ===
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# === Cursor-specific exclusions ===
# Prevent AI from reading sensitive files
*.key
*.pem
*.crt
*.csr
*.env
secrets/
config/secrets/


How to Use

For Git: Save this as .gitignore in your project root.
For Cursor AI: Save the same content as .cursorignore in your project root.
Cursor will then ignore these files when providing AI assistance.


💡 Tip:
If you want to exclude all virtual environments regardless of name, you can add:
Gitignore*/venv/
*/.venv/
*/env/
*/.env/

This catches nested venv folders inside subdirectories.

